"P.L. 1999, c.006 (A2008 1R) (https://pub.njleg.gov/bills/9899/AL99/6_.HTM)\nciteturn8search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.7 years ago; Crawled: 27.7 years ago; Be It Enacted by the Senate and General Assembly of the State of New Jersey: ... Every employer shall pay to each of his employees wages at a rate of not less than [$3.80 per hour as of the effective date of P.L.1990, c.18, $4.25 per hour as of April 1, 1991 and] $5.05 per hour as of April 1, 1992 and, after ^{1}[July 1, 1998] January 1, 1999^{1}, the minimum hourly wage rate set by section 6(a)(1) of the federal \"Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938\" (29 U.S.C. s.206(a)(1)) for 40 hours of working time in any week and 1 1/2 times such employee's regular hourly wage for each hour of working time in excess of 40 hours in any week, except this overtime rate shall not include any individual employed in a bona fide executive, administrative, or professional capacity or, if an applicable wage order has been issued by the commissioner under section 17 (C.34:11-56a16) of this act, not less than the wages prescribed in said order.\nP.L. 1999, CHAPTER 6, approved January 21, 1999\n\nAssembly, No. 2008 (First Reprint)\n\nAn Act concerning the minimum wage and amending P.L.1996, c.113.\n\nBe It Enacted by the Senate and General Assembly of the State of New Jersey:\n\n1. Section 5 of P.L.1966, c.113 (C.34:11-56a4) is amended to read as follows:\n\n5. Every employer shall pay to each of his employees wages at a rate of not less than [$3.80 per hour as of the effective date of P.L.1990, c.18, $4.25 per hour as of April 1, 1991 and] $5.05 per hour as of April 1, 1992 and, after ^{1}[July 1, 1998] January 1, 1999^{1}, the minimum hourly wage rate set by section 6(a)(1) of the federal \"Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938\" (29 U.S.C. s.206(a)(1)) for 40 hours of working time in any week and 1 1/2 times such employee's regular hourly wage for each hour of working time in excess of 40 hours in any week, except this overtime rate shall not include any individual employed in a bona fide executive, administrative, or professional capacity or, if an applicable wage order has been issued by the commissioner under section 17 (C.34:11-56a16) of this act, not less than the wages prescribed in said order. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNJ Legislature (https://www.njleg.state.nj.us/bill-search/2008/A699/bill-text?f=A1000&n=699_I1)\nciteturn8search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Every employer shall pay to each of his employees wages at a rate of not less than $5.05 per hour as of April 1, 1992 and, after January 1, 1999 the federal minimum hourly wage rate set by section 6(a)(1) of the federal \"Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938\" (29 U.S.C. s.206(a)(1)) for 40 hours of working time in any week and 1 1/2 times such employee's regular hourly wage for each hour of working time in excess of 40 hours in any week, except this overtime rate shall not include any individual employed in a bona fide executive, administrative, or professional capacity or, if an applicable wage order has been issued by the commissioner under section 17 (C.34:11-56a16) of this act, not less than the wages prescribed in said order. ...      This bill increases New Jersey's minimum wage rate and establishes a new method of determining the minimum wage in the future.\n\n## Bill A699\n\n### Session 2008 - 2009\n\nA699\n\nASSEMBLY, No. 699\n\nSTATE OF NEW JERSEY\n\n213th LEGISLATURE\n\n \n\nPRE-FILED FOR INTRODUCTION IN THE 2008 SESSION\n\nSponsored by:\n\nAssemblywoman L. GRACE SPENCER\n\nDistrict 29 (Essex and Union)\n\nSYNOPSIS\n\n     Increases minimum wage.\n\n \n\nCURRENT VERSION OF TEXT\n\n     Introduced Pending Technical Review by Legislative Counsel\n\nAn Act increasing the minimum wage and amending P.L.1966, c.113.\n\n \n\n     Be It Enacted by the Senate and General Assembly of the State of New Jersey:\n\n \n\n     1.  Section 5 of P.L.1966, c.113 (C.34:11-56a4) is amended to read as follows:\n\n     5.  Every employer shall pay to each of his employees wages at a rate of not less than $5.05 per hour as of April 1, 1992 and, after January 1, 1999 the federal minimum hourly wage rate set by section 6(a)(1) of the federal \"Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938\" (29 U.S.C. s.206(a)(1)) for 40 hours of working time in any week and 1 1/2 times such employee's regular hourly wage for each hour of working time in excess of 40 hours in any week, except this overtime rate shall not include any individual employed in a bona fide executive, administrative, or professional capacity or, if an applicable wage order has been issued by the commissioner under section 17 (C.34:11-56a16) of this act, not less than the wages prescribed in said order.  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY CHECKLIST (https://repo.njstatelib.org/server/api/core/bitstreams/da0ecddd-a999-4850-8969-317324d0f197/content)\nciteturn8search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.1 years ago; $3.85 on April 1, 1990, $4.25 on April 1, 1991, and $5.05 on April 1, ... 1992 will do nothing more than to raise the value of the minimum ... reasonable for New Jersey's minimum wage to be higher than the\n, ~ \nASSEMBLY LABOR COMMITTEE \nSTATEMENT TO \nASSEMBLY, No. 13 \nwith committee amendments \nSTATE OF NEW JERSEY  \nDATED: MARCH 15, 1990 \nThe Assembly Labor Committee reports favorably Assembly Bill \nNo. 13 with committee amendments. \nAs amended, this bill increases incrementally the State's \nminimum wage rate from the current minimum of $3.35 per hour to \n$3.85 on April 1, 1990, $4.25 on April 1, 1991, and $5.05 on April 1, \n1992. \nAn increase in the minimum wage is needed because the value of \npresent minimum wage has been severely eroded by inflation since it \nwas last increased in 1981. The present minimum wage of $3.35 \npermits a full-time worker to eam less than $7,000 per year, well \nbelow the official federal poverty level for a household of two. By \npermitting such low wages, the State sends an unfortunate message \nregarding the importance of the work ethic. The increase to $5.05 in \n1992 will do nothing more than to raise the value of the minimum \nwage closer to the inflation-adjusted value it had in 1981. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTable Data - State Minimum Wage Rate for New Jersey | FRED | St. Louis Fed (https://fred.stlouisfed.org/data/STTMINWGNJ)\nciteturn8search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # Table Data - State Minimum Wage Rate for New Jersey ... 1992-01-01  | 4.25\n\n# Table Data - State Minimum Wage Rate for New Jersey\n\nTitle  | State Minimum Wage Rate for New Jersey\nSeries ID  | STTMINWGNJ\nSource  | U.S. Department of Labor\nRelease  | Minimum Wage Rate by State (Not a Press Release)\nSeasonal Adjustment  | Not Seasonally Adjusted\nFrequency  | Annual\nUnits  | Dollars per Hour\nDate Range  | 1968-01-01 to 2026-01-01\nLast Updated  | 2026-01-01 1:34 PM CST\nDATE  | VALUE\n--- | ---\n1968-01-01  | 1.40\n1969-01-01  | 1.40\n1970-01-01  | 1.50\n1971-01-01  | 1.50\n1972-01-01  | 1.50\n1973-01-01  | 1.50\n1974-01-01  | 1.50\n1975-01-01  | 1.50\n1976-01-01  | 2.20\n1977-01-01  | 2.20\n1978-01-01  | 2.20\n1979-01-01  | 2.50\n1980-01-01  | 3.10\n1981-01-01  | 3.35\n1982-01-01  | 3.35\n1983-01-01  | 3.35\n1984-01-01  | 3.35\n1985-01-01  | 3.35\n1986-01-01  | 3.35\n1987-01-01  | 3.35\n1988-01-01  | 3.35\n1989-01-01  | 3.35\n1990-01-01  | 3.35\n1991-01-01  | 3.80\n1992-01-01  | 4.25\n1993-01-01  | 4.25\n1994-01-01  | 5.05\n1995-01-01  | 5.05\n1996-01-01  | 5.05\n1997-01-01  | 5.05\n1998-01-01  | 5.05\n1999-01-01  | 5.05\n2000-01-01  | 5.15\n2001-01-01  | 5.15\n2002-01-01  | 5.15\n2003-01-01  | 5.15\n2004-01-01  | 5.15\n2005-01-01  | 5.15\n2006-01-01  | 6.15\n2007-01-01  | 7.15\n2008-01-01  | 7.15\n2009-01-01  | 7.15\n2010-01-01  | 7.25\n2011-01-01  | 7.25\n2012-01-01  | 7.25\n2013-01-01  | 7